    "['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system authentication-order tacplus password', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system authentication-order tacplus password', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration for the Cisco AAA authentication command:\\n\\n##\\nset system authentication-order tacplus password\\n##\\n\\nThis Juniper command:\\n1. Sets the authentication order to first try TACACS+ (tacplus)\\n2. Falls back to local password authentication if TACACS+ is unavailable\\n3. Maintains the same authentication behavior as the Cisco command where it first attempts TACACS+ authentication and then local authentication as a fallback\", 'trans_template': 'set system authentication-order tacplus password'}]]": 1,
    "['aaa authorization exec [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login tacplus accounting events login', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system login tacplus accounting events login', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `aaa authorization exec default group tacacs+ local` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system login tacplus accounting events login\\nset system login tacplus authorization\\nset system login user * authentication-order [ tacplus password ]\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper configuration accomplishes the same authorization behavior by:\\n  1. Enabling TACACS+ accounting for login events\\n  2. Enabling TACACS+ authorization\\n  3. Setting the authentication order to try TACACS+ first, then local passwords\\n- The `*` wildcard applies this to all users\\n- This maintains the same fallback to local authentication if TACACS+ is unavailable\", 'trans_template': 'set system login tacplus accounting events login'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login tacplus authorization', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system login tacplus authorization', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `aaa authorization exec default group tacacs+ local` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system login tacplus accounting events login\\nset system login tacplus authorization\\nset system login user * authentication-order [ tacplus password ]\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper configuration accomplishes the same authorization behavior by:\\n  1. Enabling TACACS+ accounting for login events\\n  2. Enabling TACACS+ authorization\\n  3. Setting the authentication order to try TACACS+ first, then local passwords\\n- The `*` wildcard applies this to all users\\n- This maintains the same fallback to local authentication if TACACS+ is unavailable\", 'trans_template': 'set system login tacplus authorization'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login user * authentication-order [ tacplus password ]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system login user * authentication-order [ tacplus password ]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `aaa authorization exec default group tacacs+ local` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system login tacplus accounting events login\\nset system login tacplus authorization\\nset system login user * authentication-order [ tacplus password ]\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper configuration accomplishes the same authorization behavior by:\\n  1. Enabling TACACS+ accounting for login events\\n  2. Enabling TACACS+ authorization\\n  3. Setting the authentication order to try TACACS+ first, then local passwords\\n- The `*` wildcard applies this to all users\\n- This maintains the same fallback to local authentication if TACACS+ is unavailable\", 'trans_template': 'set system login user * authentication-order [ tacplus password ]'}]]": 1,
    "['aaa authorization network [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system authentication-order tacplus password', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system authentication-order tacplus password', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the Cisco `aaa authorization network default group tacacs+ local` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system authentication-order tacplus password\\nset system tacplus-server 1.1.2.1 secret \"$secret\" source-address 1.1.3.1\\nset system tacplus-options authorization\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct 1:1 mapping for this Cisco command, so this is the functional equivalent.\\n2. You would need to:\\n   - Replace \"$secret\" with your actual TACACS+ shared secret\\n   - Replace \"1.1.2.1\" with your actual TACACS+ server IP\\n   - Replace \"1.1.3.1\" with your device\\'s source IP for TACACS+ communications\\n3. The \"local\" fallback is implied in Juniper\\'s authentication-order statement by including \"password\" (local authentication) after \"tacplus\"', 'trans_template': 'set system authentication-order tacplus password'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system tacplus-server 1.1.2.1 secret \"$secret\" source-address 1.1.3.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system tacplus-server 1.1.2.1 secret \"$secret\" source-address 1.1.3.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the Cisco `aaa authorization network default group tacacs+ local` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system authentication-order tacplus password\\nset system tacplus-server 1.1.2.1 secret \"$secret\" source-address 1.1.3.1\\nset system tacplus-options authorization\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct 1:1 mapping for this Cisco command, so this is the functional equivalent.\\n2. You would need to:\\n   - Replace \"$secret\" with your actual TACACS+ shared secret\\n   - Replace \"1.1.2.1\" with your actual TACACS+ server IP\\n   - Replace \"1.1.3.1\" with your device\\'s source IP for TACACS+ communications\\n3. The \"local\" fallback is implied in Juniper\\'s authentication-order statement by including \"password\" (local authentication) after \"tacplus\"', 'trans_template': 'set system tacplus-server 1.1.2.1 secret \"$secret\" source-address 1.1.3.1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system tacplus-options authorization', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system tacplus-options authorization', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the Cisco `aaa authorization network default group tacacs+ local` command:\\n\\n##\\nset system authentication-order tacplus password\\nset system tacplus-server 1.1.2.1 secret \"$secret\" source-address 1.1.3.1\\nset system tacplus-options authorization\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct 1:1 mapping for this Cisco command, so this is the functional equivalent.\\n2. You would need to:\\n   - Replace \"$secret\" with your actual TACACS+ shared secret\\n   - Replace \"1.1.2.1\" with your actual TACACS+ server IP\\n   - Replace \"1.1.3.1\" with your device\\'s source IP for TACACS+ communications\\n3. The \"local\" fallback is implied in Juniper\\'s authentication-order statement by including \"password\" (local authentication) after \"tacplus\"', 'trans_template': 'set system tacplus-options authorization'}]]": 1,
    "['username [parameter1] secret [parameter2] [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system login user nms2-admin class super-user authentication encrypted-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system login user nms2-admin class super-user authentication encrypted-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset system login user nms2-admin class super-user authentication encrypted-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. Juniper uses `set system login user` to create local users\\n2. `class super-user` grants administrative privileges (equivalent to Cisco\\'s privilege level 15)\\n3. `authentication encrypted-password` preserves the existing MD5 hashed password\\n4. The password hash is kept exactly the same since both platforms use the same MD5 hashing algorithm for type 5 passwords', 'trans_template': 'set system login user nms2-admin class super-user authentication encrypted-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\"'}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-server user [parameter1] [parameter2] v3 auth [parameter3] [parameter4] priv [parameter5] [parameter6] [parameter7]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\" privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"at{pQGh!uMyq..8WSJ(<bU`~K_)[f-}s&*Gmw!iE\"', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\" privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"at{pQGh!uMyq..8WSJ(<bU`~K_)[f-}s&*Gmw!iE\"', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco SNMPv3 user configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\" privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"at{pQGh!uMyq..8WSJ(<bU`~K_)[f-}s&*Gmw!iE\"\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t have an exact equivalent for the \"localizedkey\" parameter - this is implicitly handled in Junos\\n2. The authentication and privacy passwords are simplified since Juniper doesn\\'t use the same complex wrapping format as Cisco\\n3. The group association (grp1) would need to be configured separately in Juniper\\n4. The actual passwords would need to match what was configured on the Cisco device for proper functionality', 'trans_template': 'set snmp v3 usm local-engine user nms2-admin authentication-sha authentication-password \"$1$gXnG$Oa1s4f1Sx6lQ4z1mGAb7Q1\" privacy-aes128 privacy-password \"at{pQGh!uMyq..8WSJ(<bU`~K_)[f-}s&*Gmw!iE\"'}]]": 1,
